-- corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-current-tax-assets-and-refunds
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Current tax is the tax due on one year's taxable profit. When the tax already paid for a period exceeds the tax due, PSAK 212 recognises the excess as an asset, a <em>current tax asset</em>. In Indonesia it arises mainly from instalments and withholding, because the law allows no loss carryback.</p></li><li><p>The asset is measured at the amount expected to be recovered from the tax authority. Where the authority disputes the claim, ISAK 123 decides how much stays. The claim is kept in full if acceptance is probable and is otherwise measured at the most likely amount or the expected value. The contingent-asset rule of PSAK 237 does not apply to income tax.</p></li><li><p>The split between current and non-current follows the expected date of cash. A claim that goes through examination, objection and appeal can take years, so contested claims move into non-current assets.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current portion, of 26,288 at 31 December 2024 against 12,503 a year earlier, and a non-current portion of 3,106 against 3,460, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; Note 43a, Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168; "Daftar" is the printed page). Together they are 29,394, or 0.10 percent of total assets (0.05 percent a year earlier); the current portion is 0.33 percent of current assets. On the other side, PHE owes corporate and dividend taxes of 238,034, all current, against 582,109 a year earlier, of which 16,086 was non-current (Note 43b, Daftar 5/152, PDF p. 169). This page covers the payable lines too.</p><p>After reading this page a student should be able to explain where a current tax asset comes from, record it from instalment to refund, measure a disputed claim, and classify it between current and non-current.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.</p><h2>1. Current tax: the amount due for the year</h2><p>Kieso's chapter on income taxes starts with the two measures of profit. <em>Accounting profit</em> is measured under SAK for investors; <em>taxable profit</em> is measured under the tax law and determines the tax the state collects. PSAK 212 <em>Income Taxes</em> (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) calls the tax on taxable profit <em>current tax</em> (para. 5). Current tax unpaid at the reporting date is a liability; tax paid in excess of the amount due is an asset (para. 12). The companion page on deferred tax assets covers the gap between the two profits. This page stays with the current part.</p><p>The line's name comes from how upstream contractors are taxed. PHE explains that contractors under production sharing contracts (PSCs) pay tax on their share of production, less bonuses, "at a combined rate comprising corporate income tax and dividend tax" (Note 2a(ii), Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26). The second part is a tax on profit after corporate tax, levied whether or not the profit is distributed. For a foreign company operating through an Indonesian branch it takes the form of <em>branch profit tax</em> under article 26 of the Income Tax Law.</p><p>For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary whose PSC was signed when the corporate rate was 25 percent and fixes a 20 percent dividend tax computes its 2024 taxable income as 100,000.</p><blockquote><p>Corporate income tax = 100,000 × 25% = 25,000</p><p>Dividend tax = (100,000 − 25,000) × 20% = 15,000</p><p>Current tax due for 2024 = 40,000, a combined rate of 40%</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>31 Dec 2024: Dr Current tax expense 40,000 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes payable 40,000</p></blockquote><p>If nothing had been paid during the year, the 40,000 would be a liability due with the annual return. The general Indonesian rate is now 22 percent; a cost-recovery PSC keeps the rate of its signing date until it is extended or amended, which PHE's policy also states for deferred tax (Note 3s, Daftar 5/69, PDF p. 86).</p><h2>2. How a current tax asset arises</h2><p>Tax is rarely paid in one sum after the year closes. It is paid during the year, before taxable profit is known, and the year-end computation then settles the difference. PHE describes its current tax as computed on "estimated taxable income", to be adjusted when the annual return is submitted (Note 43d, Daftar 5/153, PDF p. 170).</p><p>Three routes lead to an overpayment.</p><ul><li><p><em>Instalments.</em> A general Indonesian company pays monthly instalments (PPh article 25) of roughly one-twelfth of last year's tax, so a weaker year ends overpaid. Upstream contractors pay under their own regime. PMK 81/2024 lists the taxes a contractor pays as instalments during the year, the corporate tax due at year end, and the tax on income after corporate tax, paid monthly or annually. A contractor whose provisional figures overstate the year's profit, because prices fell or recoverable costs rose late in the year, ends with an overpayment.</p></li><li><p><em>Withholding.</em> Tax withheld by customers or collected on imports (PPh articles 22 and 23) is a credit against the year's tax. A company with many withheld receipts and low profit can be overpaid every year.</p></li><li><p><em>Assessments paid while disputed.</em> A company that pays an assessment it is contesting holds an asset if it expects to win (section 5).</p></li></ul><p>For example (illustration), the subsidiary of section 1 pays monthly instalments based on provisional figures. Side by side, a year that ends underpaid and one that ends overpaid:</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Underpaid (kurang bayar)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overpaid (lebih bayar)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Instalments paid during 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12 × 3,000 = 36,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12 × 3,500 = 42,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Prepaid taxes / Cr Cash</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>36,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>42,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Tax due for 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>40,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>40,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year end</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Current tax expense 40,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 36,000, Cr Corporate and dividend taxes payable 4,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Current tax expense 40,000, Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 2,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 42,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,000 payable with the annual return</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,000 receivable</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Overpayment = 42,000 − 40,000 = 2,000</p></blockquote><p>Tax expense is 40,000 in both columns. The timing of payment never changes the expense; it decides only whether a payable or a receivable is left. PSAK 212 sets no probability threshold for the overpaid 2,000, because the cash has left. The uncertainty is about the amount due, which section 4 takes up.</p><h2>3. Loss carryback and the Indonesian alternative</h2><p>Kieso's chapter produces a tax receivable from a different source, the loss carryback. A company with a loss applies it against the taxable profit of the two previous years and claims a refund of the tax paid on them. PSAK 212 recognises that benefit as an asset in the year of the loss, because it is probable and measurable (paras. 13–14).</p><p>Indonesia allows no carryback. A loss can only be carried forward five years (UU PPh art. 6(2)), and that benefit is a deferred tax asset. What does happen in a loss year is that instalments paid during it become refundable. For example (illustration), a subsidiary outside PSC activities pays 9,000 of instalments in 2024 on provisional profit, and the year ends with a tax loss of 50,000.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Kieso (with carryback)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Indonesia</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Tax paid in the two prior years</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15,000, refundable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>not refundable</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Instalments paid in 2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>refundable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,000 refundable</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Entry for the refund</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Income tax refund receivable / Cr Benefit due to loss carryback</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 9,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 9,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Loss carried forward</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>deferred tax asset</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>deferred tax asset of 50,000 × 22% = 11,000, if profit is probable</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The receivable is 9,000 because nothing is due for a loss year. The 15,000 paid in earlier years stays with the state.</p><h2>4. Measuring the claim: examination and assessment</h2><p>A current tax asset is measured at the amount expected to be recovered from the tax authority, using the rates and law enacted at the reporting date (para. 46). For an overpayment that amount is decided by the Directorate General of Taxes (DJP) through a fixed sequence under the tax procedure law (UU KUP).</p><ol><li><p><em>Annual return.</em> A company files within four months of year end, so by 30 April for a calendar year. A return showing an overpayment carries a refund request (<em>restitusi</em>).</p></li><li><p><em>Examination.</em> A refund request generally triggers a tax examination. DJP must issue an assessment within 12 months of the complete request, or the request is deemed granted (art. 17B). Low-risk taxpayers can receive a preliminary refund without examination (arts. 17C–17D).</p></li><li><p><em>Assessment.</em> An SKPLB confirms an overpayment, an SKPN finds neither overpayment nor underpayment, and an SKPKB assesses an underpayment with interest. An SKPLB is refunded after DJP offsets any other tax owed.</p></li></ol><p>The examiners' corrections set the amount. For example (illustration), the subsidiary's return for 2023 showed the same 2,000 overpayment. It was filed in April 2024 and examined during 2024, and DJP disallows 3,000 of costs.</p><blockquote><p>Tax effect of the corrections = 3,000 × 40% = 1,200</p><p>SKPLB = 2,000 − 1,200 = 800</p></blockquote><p>Had DJP disallowed 6,000 of costs, the tax effect of 2,400 would exceed the claim, and the result would be an SKPKB for 2,400 − 2,000 = 400 plus interest. A refund claim can turn into an underpayment.</p><h2>5. Uncertainty: measuring a disputed claim under ISAK 123</h2><p>If the company accepts the corrections, the asset falls to 800 and 1,200 is charged to current tax expense. If it contests them, the question is how much of the claim is still "in excess of the amount due". Kieso's chapter on current liabilities lists a <em>possible tax refund</em> among contingent assets, recognised only when virtually certain under PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). For income tax that rule does not apply. PSAK 237 excludes income taxes (para. 5), and in July 2014 the IFRS Interpretations Committee confirmed that IAS 12 governs recognition. An asset is recognised if the cash paid exceeds the tax expected to be due, and the reference to IAS 37 in IAS 12 para. 88 concerns disclosure only.</p><p>How much tax is "expected to be due" when the treatment is disputed is answered by ISAK 123 <em>Uncertainty over Income Tax Treatments</em> (formerly ISAK 34, IFRIC 23).</p><ol><li><p>Assume the authority examines the item with full knowledge of the facts. The chance that it goes unnoticed does not count.</p></li><li><p>Ask whether the authority, including the courts, will probably accept the treatment used in the return.</p></li><li><p>If acceptance is probable, measure the tax as filed (para. 10).</p></li><li><p>If not, use the method that better predicts the outcome (para. 11): the <em>most likely amount</em>, the single most likely result, suited to an all-or-nothing dispute; or the <em>expected value</em>, the probability-weighted sum, suited to a range of results.</p></li></ol><p>For example (illustration), the subsidiary contests all 3,000 in two items.</p><ul><li><p><em>Item A</em>, 1,750 of head-office overhead charged under an allocation method SKK Migas approved, tax effect 700. The company has won the same point in court for an earlier year and judges acceptance probable. It keeps 700 in full.</p></li><li><p><em>Item B</em>, 1,250 of well costs DJP says served an affiliate's block, tax effect 500. Without precedent the company puts its chance at 40 percent. Acceptance is not probable, and the court will allow the costs or not.</p></li></ul><blockquote><p>Most likely amount for item B = 0; expected value would be 40% × 500 = 200, a result that cannot occur</p><p>Asset carried = 800 (SKPLB) + 700 (item A) = 1,500</p><p>Charge = 2,000 − 1,500 = 500</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Current tax expense (change in estimate for 2023 tax) 500 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 500</p></blockquote><p>The examination is new information, so the reduction is a change in estimate in the year it arises, with no restatement (PSAK 208, formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8). The company still objects to the full 1,200. If it later wins item B, the 500 reduces tax expense when acceptance becomes probable.</p><p>The same test applies when a company pays a disputed SKPKB. Since the HPP law, tax unpaid when an objection is filed is not a tax debt while the objection runs, but a lost objection or appeal carries a fine of 30 or 60 percent of the tax still unpaid (section 6). Paying first removes that base. For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays an SKPKB of 900 before objecting, on grounds it judges probable to succeed.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable (assessment in dispute) 900 / Cr Cash 900</p></blockquote><p>The cash paid is certain, the tax due is not, and the excess of one over the other is the asset.</p><h2>6. Interest and penalties</h2><p>When a taxpayer wins an objection or appeal, DJP pays <em>imbalan bunga</em>, interest compensation, for the period from the assessment to the decision, up to 24 months (UU KUP art. 27B). When it loses, fines apply: 30 percent of the tax due under a rejected objection and 60 percent under a rejected appeal, computed on tax not yet paid. A refund dispute where no tax is unpaid gives little or no base for the fine.</p><p>Whether interest is income tax calls for a judgement, and the entity has no free policy choice between the two standards. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in 2017 that an entity applies IAS 12 to interest and penalties it judges to be income taxes, and IAS 37 otherwise, and discloses the judgement under IAS 1 para. 122. Under PSAK 237, interest compensation would be a contingent asset, recognised only when virtually certain.</p><h2>7. Presentation: current or non-current, gross or net</h2><p><strong>Current or non-current.</strong> An asset is current when the entity expects to realise it within twelve months of the reporting date (PSAK 201.66, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). For a tax claim the test is the expected date of cash, and the dispute timetable sets it.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Stage</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Statutory time</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Running total from filing</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Examination of a refund request</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>up to 12 months</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12 months</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Objection, filed within 3 months; decided within 12</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>up to 15 months</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>27 months</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Appeal to the Tax Court, filed within 3 months; decided within 12, extendable by 3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>up to 18 months</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>45 months</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Judicial review (<em>peninjauan kembali</em>) at the Supreme Court, filed within 3 months; decided within 6</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>up to 9 months</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>54 months</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>For example (illustration), at 31 December 2024 the subsidiary's 1,500 splits as follows.</p><blockquote><p>SKPLB of 800 issued, refund expected in early 2025: current</p><p>Item A of 700, objection to be filed in 2025, DJP expected to reject it as it did for the earlier year, and the appeal expected to run to 2027: non-current</p><p>Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, non-current 700 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current 700</p></blockquote><p>One tax year now sits in both lines. A judicial review by DJP does not suspend a Tax Court decision, so a won appeal can be refunded before the Supreme Court rules. Each new contested year adds a layer, so the non-current part grows while disputes start faster than the courts close them.</p><p><strong>The payable side.</strong> Current tax still unpaid is a liability (para. 12), measured at the amount expected to be paid (para. 46), and ISAK 123 measures an uncertain treatment the same way on either side. It is current unless, at the reporting date, the entity has a right to defer settlement for at least twelve months (PSAK 201.69(d)). A payment schedule granted by DJP, or tax that by law is not yet due while an objection runs, could give that right. For example (illustration), the underpaid column of section 2 leaves 4,000 payable with the annual return by 30 April 2025, which is current.</p><p><strong>Gross or net.</strong> A current tax asset and a current tax liability are offset only if the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 212.71). In consolidated statements one subsidiary's receivable can be set against another's payable only if they have that right and intention together (para. 73). Indonesia has no group taxation, and each PSC usually files in its own subsidiary. So a group can show a receivable and a payable for the same kind of tax at the same date. For example (illustration):</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subsidiary A</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Subsidiary B</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Group</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Overpaid 2024 tax</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,000 receivable</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Underpaid 2024 tax</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000 payable</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Shown net?</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no, different taxpayers</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Distinct from deferred tax.</strong> A current tax asset is cash already paid that the state must return, whatever the company earns next year. A deferred tax asset is a future deduction worth something only if there is future taxable profit. The first can be current or non-current; the second is always non-current (PSAK 201.56).</p><h2>8. Taxes receivable across industries</h2><p>The theory is the same everywhere. What differs is which taxes are income taxes, and which industries are routinely overpaid.</p><p><strong>Final-tax industries: construction, real estate, deposits.</strong> Income taxed under a final regime is outside PSAK 212, as PHE's policy notes for its own final-taxed items (Note 3s, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). A contractor whose customers withhold final tax on construction services has no income tax receivable on that revenue. The tax withheld is the final tax, an expense or a deduction from revenue, not an income tax overpayment. For example (illustration), construction revenue of 1,000 with final tax withheld at 2.65 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Final tax = 1,000 × 2.65% = 26.5</p><p>Dr Final tax expense 26.5 / Cr Trade receivables 26.5</p></blockquote><p><strong>Exporters and VAT.</strong> Exporters charge 0 percent VAT on exports but pay VAT on inputs, so they hold VAT refund claims. VAT is not an income tax, so the claim is an <em>other tax receivable</em> outside PSAK 212 and ISAK 123. Nor is it a financial asset under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), because it arises from statute, not from a contract (PSAK 232 AG12, IAS 32). It is still an asset. In January 2019 the IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded that a deposit paid on a disputed tax other than income tax is an asset, not a contingent asset, accounted for by analogy under PSAK 208. PHE shows the size of this neighbour. Reimbursable VAT of 505,861 sits inside "other taxes receivable", against 29,394 of corporate and dividend taxes receivable (Note 43a). Its separate provision for impairment of tax receivable, 10,555 in both years, sits in the other-taxes line.</p><p><strong>Manufacturers and traders with import withholding.</strong> PPh 22 collected on imports is credited against annual tax. A low-margin importer can be overpaid year after year, which is why the preliminary refund route of arts. 17C–17D exists. These companies carry a recurring, mostly current, tax receivable.</p><p><strong>Groups with foreign branches.</strong> A foreign company's Indonesian branch, a <em>bentuk usaha tetap</em> (BUT), pays branch profit tax on after-tax profit, sometimes at a treaty rate. Disputes over that tax produce receivables of the kind section 9 shows at PHE.</p><h2>9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 43a and 43g</h2><p>Note 43a gives the composition (Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>26,288</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,503</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,106</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,460</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>29,394</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>15,963</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Corporate and dividend taxes payable, current (Note 43b)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>238,034</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>566,023</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Corporate and dividend taxes payable, non-current (Note 43b)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>–</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>16,086</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total payable</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>238,034</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>582,109</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Current portion grew 26,288 − 12,503 = 13,785, or 110.3 percent</p><p>Receivable to payable = 29,394 / 238,034 = 12.3%</p><p>Payable to 2024 current tax = 238,034 / 1,473,547 = 16.2% (Note 43c, Daftar 5/152, PDF p. 169)</p></blockquote><p>The payable fell 344,075 in 2024, and at year end it is about one-sixth of the year's current tax, which is consistent with most of it being settled during the year through the instalments of section 2. The non-current payable of 16,086 at the end of 2023 is gone by 2024. PHE does not say what it was or what right to defer it rested on.</p><p>All of the receivable belongs to subsidiaries, and the group shows it beside a payable eight times its size at the same date. That fits section 7's gross presentation if the overpaid and the underpaid amounts belong to different taxpayers or different years, which the note does not break down. PHE does not split the current portion between overpaid 2024 tax and earlier claims, so the doubling cannot be explained.</p><p>Note 43g lists the disputes. One subsidiary, which PHE calls Natuna (PSC A, Natuna Sea), shows how long a dividend-tax claim takes. For its 2018 branch profit tax it reported an overpayment of USD 7,628,906 (full amount).</p><blockquote><p>September 2021: SKP Nihil, DJP finds no overpayment</p><p>28 January 2022: objection filed; rejected 8 December 2022</p><p>1 March 2023: appeal to the Tax Court; granted in full 5 December 2023</p><p>14 March 2024: DJP applies for judicial review; Supreme Court rejects it, decision dated 30 September 2024</p></blockquote><p>The English column dates the decision 31 December 2024 (Note 43g, Daftar 5/165, PDF p. 182). Either way the claim for tax year 2018 was decided in its taxpayer's favour nearly six years after the year ended. The same table lists overpayments for 2019 to 2022 still in objection, appeal or judicial review at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/162–5/167, PDF pp. 179–184). In USD thousand, from the note's table:</p><blockquote><p>Open claims for 2019–2022 = 4,851 + 4,263 + 6,329 + 7,246 = 22,689, against a line of 29,394</p><p>Claims for 2018–2021 at the end of 2023 = 7,629 + 4,851 + 4,263 + 6,329 = 23,072, against a line of 15,963</p></blockquote><p>At the end of 2023 the Natuna claims alone exceeded the whole line, so they cannot all have been carried in it at full value. Branch profit tax is PPh article 26, and some may sit in other taxes receivable, at a lower amount, or not at all. The note does not say which.</p><h2>10. Upstream note: PSC tax</h2><p>Under a cost-recovery PSC the corporate and dividend tax rates are fixed by the contract, and costs disallowed for cost recovery by SKK Migas can be the same costs DJP corrects for tax, which can put one cost in dispute twice. Gross split PSCs pay tax under the prevailing law, and DJP can assess them for ten years instead of five (Note 43f, Daftar 5/156, PDF p. 173). That longer window keeps assessments, and the receivables they create, open for longer.</p><h2>11. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-non-current</code>: the contested part of the same claims, covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><code>corporate-dividend-taxes-payable-current</code> and <code>corporate-dividend-taxes-payable-non-current</code>: the underpaid side, shown gross and covered on this page.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-receivable-current</a>: VAT and other non-income taxes, outside PSAK 212.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a>: future deductions, as opposed to tax already paid.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">income-tax-expense-net</a>: where corrections and estimate changes are charged.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The monthly instalment basis for upstream contractors under PMK 81/2024 was seen only through a DDTC summary that lists the payment types without the formula. That provisional figures drive overpayments is this page's reading of PHE's Note 43d.</p></li><li><p>The objection, appeal, fine and interest rules of UU KUP (arts. 25–27, 27B) were checked only in secondary sources, and the judicial-review filing period in summaries that do not settle the article number. The 12-month refund rule of art. 17B was checked in DDTC and Ortax summaries.</p></li><li><p>That a judicial review by DJP does not suspend the Tax Court decision, so a won appeal can be refunded before the Supreme Court rules, is from a DDTC summary; when refunds are paid in practice was not checked.</p></li><li><p>Where the Natuna claims are carried is not stated by PHE. The page shows only that, at the end of 2023, they exceeded the whole line. Nor does PHE explain its 2023 non-current tax payable of 16,086; the deferral rights suggested for it in section 7 are possibilities under PSAK 201.69(d), not facts.</p></li><li><p>Treating a VAT refund claim by analogy with the 2019 agenda decision, which concerns a deposit on a disputed non-income tax, is a reading. An undisputed VAT overpayment is the same kind of statutory right, but the decision does not address it directly.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 2a(ii) (Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26); Note 3s (Daftar 5/68–5/70, PDF pp. 85–87); Note 43a–43d, 43f (Daftar 5/151–5/156, PDF pp. 168–173); Note 43g, PSC A (Natuna Sea) (Daftar 5/161–5/167, PDF pp. 178–184). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; line figures checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract).</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition</em> (Wiley), chapters on current liabilities and on accounting for income taxes, the textbook the lecture notes summarise; edition not checked. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes this series follows, "Inter 2 – Chapter 19 – Accounting for Income Taxes" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): pretax financial income and taxable income, current tax and income tax payable, loss carryback with an income tax refund receivable (Garuda, Groh). NOTES. Carryback does not apply in Indonesia.</p></li><li><p>Lecture notes, "Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): income tax payable, "kemungkinan restitusi pajak" as a contingent asset. NOTES. Its Indonesian rates (25 and 12.5 percent) and its 1 April payment date were not used; its contingent-asset treatment of tax refunds is corrected in section 5. Neither note covers ISAK 123, Indonesian procedure, presentation or the industry cases; sections 4–8 follow the standards and laws named below.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 212 <em>Pajak Penghasilan</em> (IAS 12), paras. 5, 12–14, 46, 71–73, 88. VERIFIED (web, AASB 112 text at standards.aasb.gov.au).</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decisions "Recognition of current income tax on uncertain tax position (IAS 12)", July 2014 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org PDF); "Interest and penalties related to income taxes", 2017 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org project page); "Deposits relating to taxes other than income tax (IAS 37)", January 2019 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org agenda decision page).</p></li><li><p>ISAK 123 <em>Ketidakpastian dalam Perlakuan Pajak Penghasilan</em> (IFRIC 23), paras. 10–11 and the full-knowledge assumption. VERIFIED (web, BDO and IFRS Foundation illustrative examples via search). PSAK 232 (IAS 32) AG12, statutory taxes not financial assets: VERIFIED (web, secondary: HM Treasury FRAB 128(03)). PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 5; PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 56, 66, 69(d) (as amended for classification of liabilities, effective 2024), 122; PSAK 208 (IAS 8). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>UU KUP (UU 6/1983 as amended, latest by UU 6/2023 and UU 7/2021), art. 17B. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews; Ortax). Art. 25(8)–(9) and 27(5d), unpaid disputed tax and the 30/60 percent fines: VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews; pajak.com). UU 14/2002 on the Tax Court, art. 93(1), judicial review decided within 6 months: VERIFIED (web, pasal.id). Arts. 3, 17C–17D, 26–27, 27B: NOT VERIFIED (secondary sources via the 2026-10-09 recheck).</p></li><li><p>UU PPh art. 6(2), five-year carryforward. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews). PMK 81/2024, taxes payable by upstream contractors. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews summary) for the list of payment types only. PP 9/2022, 2.65 percent final tax on construction work by medium, large or specialist contractors: VERIFIED (web, pajak.go.id; hukumonline).</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current tax is the tax due on one year's taxable profit. When the tax already paid for a period exceeds the tax due, PSAK 212 recognises the excess as an asset, a ","type":"text"},{"text":"current tax asset","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". In Indonesia it arises mainly from instalments and withholding, because the law allows no loss carryback.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The asset is measured at the amount expected to be recovered from the tax authority. Where the authority disputes the claim, ISAK 123 decides how much stays. The claim is kept in full if acceptance is probable and is otherwise measured at the most likely amount or the expected value. The contingent-asset rule of PSAK 237 does not apply to income tax.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The split between current and non-current follows the expected date of cash. A claim that goes through examination, objection and appeal can take years, so contested claims move into non-current assets.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current portion, of 26,288 at 31 December 2024 against 12,503 a year earlier, and a non-current portion of 3,106 against 3,460, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11; Note 43a, Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). Together they are 29,394, or 0.10 percent of total assets (0.05 percent a year earlier); the current portion is 0.33 percent of current assets. On the other side, PHE owes corporate and dividend taxes of 238,034, all current, against 582,109 a year earlier, of which 16,086 was non-current (Note 43b, Daftar 5/152, PDF p. 169). This page covers the payable lines too.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading this page a student should be able to explain where a current tax asset comes from, record it from instalment to refund, measure a disputed claim, and classify it between current and non-current.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Current tax: the amount due for the year","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's chapter on income taxes starts with the two measures of profit. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accounting profit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is measured under SAK for investors; ","type":"text"},{"text":"taxable profit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is measured under the tax law and determines the tax the state collects. PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Income Taxes","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 46, IAS 12) calls the tax on taxable profit ","type":"text"},{"text":"current tax","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 5). Current tax unpaid at the reporting date is a liability; tax paid in excess of the amount due is an asset (para. 12). The companion page on deferred tax assets covers the gap between the two profits. This page stays with the current part.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The line's name comes from how upstream contractors are taxed. PHE explains that contractors under production sharing contracts (PSCs) pay tax on their share of production, less bonuses, \"at a combined rate comprising corporate income tax and dividend tax\" (Note 2a(ii), Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26). The second part is a tax on profit after corporate tax, levied whether or not the profit is distributed. For a foreign company operating through an Indonesian branch it takes the form of ","type":"text"},{"text":"branch profit tax","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" under article 26 of the Income Tax Law.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a PHE subsidiary whose PSC was signed when the corporate rate was 25 percent and fixes a 20 percent dividend tax computes its 2024 taxable income as 100,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corporate income tax = 100,000 × 25% = 25,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dividend tax = (100,000 − 25,000) × 20% = 15,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current tax due for 2024 = 40,000, a combined rate of 40%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024: Dr Current tax expense 40,000 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes payable 40,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If nothing had been paid during the year, the 40,000 would be a liability due with the annual return. The general Indonesian rate is now 22 percent; a cost-recovery PSC keeps the rate of its signing date until it is extended or amended, which PHE's policy also states for deferred tax (Note 3s, Daftar 5/69, PDF p. 86).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. How a current tax asset arises","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax is rarely paid in one sum after the year closes. It is paid during the year, before taxable profit is known, and the year-end computation then settles the difference. PHE describes its current tax as computed on \"estimated taxable income\", to be adjusted when the annual return is submitted (Note 43d, Daftar 5/153, PDF p. 170).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Three routes lead to an overpayment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Instalments.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" A general Indonesian company pays monthly instalments (PPh article 25) of roughly one-twelfth of last year's tax, so a weaker year ends overpaid. Upstream contractors pay under their own regime. PMK 81/2024 lists the taxes a contractor pays as instalments during the year, the corporate tax due at year end, and the tax on income after corporate tax, paid monthly or annually. A contractor whose provisional figures overstate the year's profit, because prices fell or recoverable costs rose late in the year, ends with an overpayment.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Withholding.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" Tax withheld by customers or collected on imports (PPh articles 22 and 23) is a credit against the year's tax. A company with many withheld receipts and low profit can be overpaid every year.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Assessments paid while disputed.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" A company that pays an assessment it is contesting holds an asset if it expects to win (section 5).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the subsidiary of section 1 pays monthly instalments based on provisional figures. Side by side, a year that ends underpaid and one that ends overpaid:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Underpaid (kurang bayar)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overpaid (lebih bayar)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Instalments paid during 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12 × 3,000 = 36,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12 × 3,500 = 42,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Prepaid taxes / Cr Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"36,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"42,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax due for 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"40,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"40,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year end","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Current tax expense 40,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 36,000, Cr Corporate and dividend taxes payable 4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Current tax expense 40,000, Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 2,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 42,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,000 payable with the annual return","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,000 receivable","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overpayment = 42,000 − 40,000 = 2,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax expense is 40,000 in both columns. The timing of payment never changes the expense; it decides only whether a payable or a receivable is left. PSAK 212 sets no probability threshold for the overpaid 2,000, because the cash has left. The uncertainty is about the amount due, which section 4 takes up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Loss carryback and the Indonesian alternative","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso's chapter produces a tax receivable from a different source, the loss carryback. A company with a loss applies it against the taxable profit of the two previous years and claims a refund of the tax paid on them. PSAK 212 recognises that benefit as an asset in the year of the loss, because it is probable and measurable (paras. 13–14).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesia allows no carryback. A loss can only be carried forward five years (UU PPh art. 6(2)), and that benefit is a deferred tax asset. What does happen in a loss year is that instalments paid during it become refundable. For example (illustration), a subsidiary outside PSC activities pays 9,000 of instalments in 2024 on provisional profit, and the year ends with a tax loss of 50,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso (with carryback)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesia","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax paid in the two prior years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,000, refundable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"not refundable","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Instalments paid in 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"refundable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,000 refundable","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Entry for the refund","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Income tax refund receivable / Cr Benefit due to loss carryback","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 9,000 / Cr Prepaid taxes 9,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Loss carried forward","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred tax asset","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred tax asset of 50,000 × 22% = 11,000, if profit is probable","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The receivable is 9,000 because nothing is due for a loss year. The 15,000 paid in earlier years stays with the state.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Measuring the claim: examination and assessment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A current tax asset is measured at the amount expected to be recovered from the tax authority, using the rates and law enacted at the reporting date (para. 46). For an overpayment that amount is decided by the Directorate General of Taxes (DJP) through a fixed sequence under the tax procedure law (UU KUP).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Annual return.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" A company files within four months of year end, so by 30 April for a calendar year. A return showing an overpayment carries a refund request (","type":"text"},{"text":"restitusi","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":").","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Examination.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" A refund request generally triggers a tax examination. DJP must issue an assessment within 12 months of the complete request, or the request is deemed granted (art. 17B). Low-risk taxpayers can receive a preliminary refund without examination (arts. 17C–17D).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Assessment.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" An SKPLB confirms an overpayment, an SKPN finds neither overpayment nor underpayment, and an SKPKB assesses an underpayment with interest. An SKPLB is refunded after DJP offsets any other tax owed.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The examiners' corrections set the amount. For example (illustration), the subsidiary's return for 2023 showed the same 2,000 overpayment. It was filed in April 2024 and examined during 2024, and DJP disallows 3,000 of costs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Tax effect of the corrections = 3,000 × 40% = 1,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"SKPLB = 2,000 − 1,200 = 800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Had DJP disallowed 6,000 of costs, the tax effect of 2,400 would exceed the claim, and the result would be an SKPKB for 2,400 − 2,000 = 400 plus interest. A refund claim can turn into an underpayment.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Uncertainty: measuring a disputed claim under ISAK 123","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If the company accepts the corrections, the asset falls to 800 and 1,200 is charged to current tax expense. If it contests them, the question is how much of the claim is still \"in excess of the amount due\". Kieso's chapter on current liabilities lists a ","type":"text"},{"text":"possible tax refund","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" among contingent assets, recognised only when virtually certain under PSAK 237 (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37). For income tax that rule does not apply. PSAK 237 excludes income taxes (para. 5), and in July 2014 the IFRS Interpretations Committee confirmed that IAS 12 governs recognition. An asset is recognised if the cash paid exceeds the tax expected to be due, and the reference to IAS 37 in IAS 12 para. 88 concerns disclosure only.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"How much tax is \"expected to be due\" when the treatment is disputed is answered by ISAK 123 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Uncertainty over Income Tax Treatments","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly ISAK 34, IFRIC 23).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Assume the authority examines the item with full knowledge of the facts. The chance that it goes unnoticed does not count.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ask whether the authority, including the courts, will probably accept the treatment used in the return.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If acceptance is probable, measure the tax as filed (para. 10).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"If not, use the method that better predicts the outcome (para. 11): the ","type":"text"},{"text":"most likely amount","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the single most likely result, suited to an all-or-nothing dispute; or the ","type":"text"},{"text":"expected value","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the probability-weighted sum, suited to a range of results.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), the subsidiary contests all 3,000 in two items.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item A","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", 1,750 of head-office overhead charged under an allocation method SKK Migas approved, tax effect 700. The company has won the same point in court for an earlier year and judges acceptance probable. It keeps 700 in full.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item B","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", 1,250 of well costs DJP says served an affiliate's block, tax effect 500. Without precedent the company puts its chance at 40 percent. Acceptance is not probable, and the court will allow the costs or not.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Most likely amount for item B = 0; expected value would be 40% × 500 = 200, a result that cannot occur","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Asset carried = 800 (SKPLB) + 700 (item A) = 1,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Charge = 2,000 − 1,500 = 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Current tax expense (change in estimate for 2023 tax) 500 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The examination is new information, so the reduction is a change in estimate in the year it arises, with no restatement (PSAK 208, formerly PSAK 25, IAS 8). The company still objects to the full 1,200. If it later wins item B, the 500 reduces tax expense when acceptance becomes probable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The same test applies when a company pays a disputed SKPKB. Since the HPP law, tax unpaid when an objection is filed is not a tax debt while the objection runs, but a lost objection or appeal carries a fine of 30 or 60 percent of the tax still unpaid (section 6). Paying first removes that base. For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays an SKPKB of 900 before objecting, on grounds it judges probable to succeed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable (assessment in dispute) 900 / Cr Cash 900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash paid is certain, the tax due is not, and the excess of one over the other is the asset.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Interest and penalties","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When a taxpayer wins an objection or appeal, DJP pays ","type":"text"},{"text":"imbalan bunga","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", interest compensation, for the period from the assessment to the decision, up to 24 months (UU KUP art. 27B). When it loses, fines apply: 30 percent of the tax due under a rejected objection and 60 percent under a rejected appeal, computed on tax not yet paid. A refund dispute where no tax is unpaid gives little or no base for the fine.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Whether interest is income tax calls for a judgement, and the entity has no free policy choice between the two standards. The IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded in 2017 that an entity applies IAS 12 to interest and penalties it judges to be income taxes, and IAS 37 otherwise, and discloses the judgement under IAS 1 para. 122. Under PSAK 237, interest compensation would be a contingent asset, recognised only when virtually certain.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Presentation: current or non-current, gross or net","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current or non-current.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An asset is current when the entity expects to realise it within twelve months of the reporting date (PSAK 201.66, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1). For a tax claim the test is the expected date of cash, and the dispute timetable sets it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Stage","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Statutory time","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Running total from filing","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Examination of a refund request","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"up to 12 months","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12 months","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Objection, filed within 3 months; decided within 12","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"up to 15 months","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"27 months","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Appeal to the Tax Court, filed within 3 months; decided within 12, extendable by 3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"up to 18 months","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"45 months","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Judicial review (","type":"text"},{"text":"peninjauan kembali","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") at the Supreme Court, filed within 3 months; decided within 6","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"up to 9 months","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"54 months","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), at 31 December 2024 the subsidiary's 1,500 splits as follows.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"SKPLB of 800 issued, refund expected in early 2025: current","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item A of 700, objection to be filed in 2025, DJP expected to reject it as it did for the earlier year, and the appeal expected to run to 2027: non-current","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, non-current 700 / Cr Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current 700","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"One tax year now sits in both lines. A judicial review by DJP does not suspend a Tax Court decision, so a won appeal can be refunded before the Supreme Court rules. Each new contested year adds a layer, so the non-current part grows while disputes start faster than the courts close them.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The payable side.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Current tax still unpaid is a liability (para. 12), measured at the amount expected to be paid (para. 46), and ISAK 123 measures an uncertain treatment the same way on either side. It is current unless, at the reporting date, the entity has a right to defer settlement for at least twelve months (PSAK 201.69(d)). A payment schedule granted by DJP, or tax that by law is not yet due while an objection runs, could give that right. For example (illustration), the underpaid column of section 2 leaves 4,000 payable with the annual return by 30 April 2025, which is current.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gross or net.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A current tax asset and a current tax liability are offset only if the entity has a legally enforceable right to set them off and intends to settle net or simultaneously (PSAK 212.71). In consolidated statements one subsidiary's receivable can be set against another's payable only if they have that right and intention together (para. 73). Indonesia has no group taxation, and each PSC usually files in its own subsidiary. So a group can show a receivable and a payable for the same kind of tax at the same date. For example (illustration):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary A","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subsidiary B","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Group","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Overpaid 2024 tax","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,000 receivable","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Underpaid 2024 tax","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000 payable","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shown net?","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no, different taxpayers","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Distinct from deferred tax.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A current tax asset is cash already paid that the state must return, whatever the company earns next year. A deferred tax asset is a future deduction worth something only if there is future taxable profit. The first can be current or non-current; the second is always non-current (PSAK 201.56).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Taxes receivable across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The theory is the same everywhere. What differs is which taxes are income taxes, and which industries are routinely overpaid.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Final-tax industries: construction, real estate, deposits.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Income taxed under a final regime is outside PSAK 212, as PHE's policy notes for its own final-taxed items (Note 3s, Daftar 5/70, PDF p. 87). A contractor whose customers withhold final tax on construction services has no income tax receivable on that revenue. The tax withheld is the final tax, an expense or a deduction from revenue, not an income tax overpayment. For example (illustration), construction revenue of 1,000 with final tax withheld at 2.65 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Final tax = 1,000 × 2.65% = 26.5","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Final tax expense 26.5 / Cr Trade receivables 26.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Exporters and VAT.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Exporters charge 0 percent VAT on exports but pay VAT on inputs, so they hold VAT refund claims. VAT is not an income tax, so the claim is an ","type":"text"},{"text":"other tax receivable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" outside PSAK 212 and ISAK 123. Nor is it a financial asset under PSAK 109 (formerly PSAK 71, IFRS 9), because it arises from statute, not from a contract (PSAK 232 AG12, IAS 32). It is still an asset. In January 2019 the IFRS Interpretations Committee concluded that a deposit paid on a disputed tax other than income tax is an asset, not a contingent asset, accounted for by analogy under PSAK 208. PHE shows the size of this neighbour. Reimbursable VAT of 505,861 sits inside \"other taxes receivable\", against 29,394 of corporate and dividend taxes receivable (Note 43a). Its separate provision for impairment of tax receivable, 10,555 in both years, sits in the other-taxes line.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Manufacturers and traders with import withholding.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PPh 22 collected on imports is credited against annual tax. A low-margin importer can be overpaid year after year, which is why the preliminary refund route of arts. 17C–17D exists. These companies carry a recurring, mostly current, tax receivable.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Groups with foreign branches.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A foreign company's Indonesian branch, a ","type":"text"},{"text":"bentuk usaha tetap","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (BUT), pays branch profit tax on after-tax profit, sometimes at a treaty rate. Disputes over that tax produce receivables of the kind section 9 shows at PHE.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 43a and 43g","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 43a gives the composition (Daftar 5/151, PDF p. 168).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"26,288","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,503","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corporate and dividend taxes receivable, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,106","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,460","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"29,394","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15,963","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corporate and dividend taxes payable, current (Note 43b)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"238,034","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"566,023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corporate and dividend taxes payable, non-current (Note 43b)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"–","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"16,086","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"238,034","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"582,109","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current portion grew 26,288 − 12,503 = 13,785, or 110.3 percent","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivable to payable = 29,394 / 238,034 = 12.3%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payable to 2024 current tax = 238,034 / 1,473,547 = 16.2% (Note 43c, Daftar 5/152, PDF p. 169)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The payable fell 344,075 in 2024, and at year end it is about one-sixth of the year's current tax, which is consistent with most of it being settled during the year through the instalments of section 2. The non-current payable of 16,086 at the end of 2023 is gone by 2024. PHE does not say what it was or what right to defer it rested on.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All of the receivable belongs to subsidiaries, and the group shows it beside a payable eight times its size at the same date. That fits section 7's gross presentation if the overpaid and the underpaid amounts belong to different taxpayers or different years, which the note does not break down. PHE does not split the current portion between overpaid 2024 tax and earlier claims, so the doubling cannot be explained.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 43g lists the disputes. One subsidiary, which PHE calls Natuna (PSC A, Natuna Sea), shows how long a dividend-tax claim takes. For its 2018 branch profit tax it reported an overpayment of USD 7,628,906 (full amount).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"September 2021: SKP Nihil, DJP finds no overpayment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"28 January 2022: objection filed; rejected 8 December 2022","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 March 2023: appeal to the Tax Court; granted in full 5 December 2023","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"14 March 2024: DJP applies for judicial review; Supreme Court rejects it, decision dated 30 September 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The English column dates the decision 31 December 2024 (Note 43g, Daftar 5/165, PDF p. 182). Either way the claim for tax year 2018 was decided in its taxpayer's favour nearly six years after the year ended. The same table lists overpayments for 2019 to 2022 still in objection, appeal or judicial review at the end of 2024 (Daftar 5/162–5/167, PDF pp. 179–184). In USD thousand, from the note's table:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Open claims for 2019–2022 = 4,851 + 4,263 + 6,329 + 7,246 = 22,689, against a line of 29,394","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Claims for 2018–2021 at the end of 2023 = 7,629 + 4,851 + 4,263 + 6,329 = 23,072, against a line of 15,963","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"At the end of 2023 the Natuna claims alone exceeded the whole line, so they cannot all have been carried in it at full value. Branch profit tax is PPh article 26, and some may sit in other taxes receivable, at a lower amount, or not at all. The note does not say which.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Upstream note: PSC tax","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Under a cost-recovery PSC the corporate and dividend tax rates are fixed by the contract, and costs disallowed for cost recovery by SKK Migas can be the same costs DJP corrects for tax, which can put one cost in dispute twice. Gross split PSCs pay tax under the prevailing law, and DJP can assess them for ten years instead of five (Note 43f, Daftar 5/156, PDF p. 173). That longer window keeps assessments, and the receivables they create, open for longer.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-receivable-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the contested part of the same claims, covered on this page.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-payable-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"corporate-dividend-taxes-payable-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the underpaid side, shown gross and covered on this page.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-taxes-receivable-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": VAT and other non-income taxes, outside PSAK 212.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": future deductions, as opposed to tax already paid.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"income-tax-expense-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where corrections and estimate changes are charged.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The monthly instalment basis for upstream contractors under PMK 81/2024 was seen only through a DDTC summary that lists the payment types without the formula. That provisional figures drive overpayments is this page's reading of PHE's Note 43d.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The objection, appeal, fine and interest rules of UU KUP (arts. 25–27, 27B) were checked only in secondary sources, and the judicial-review filing period in summaries that do not settle the article number. The 12-month refund rule of art. 17B was checked in DDTC and Ortax summaries.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That a judicial review by DJP does not suspend the Tax Court decision, so a won appeal can be refunded before the Supreme Court rules, is from a DDTC summary; when refunds are paid in practice was not checked.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Where the Natuna claims are carried is not stated by PHE. The page shows only that, at the end of 2023, they exceeded the whole line. Nor does PHE explain its 2023 non-current tax payable of 16,086; the deferral rights suggested for it in section 7 are possibilities under PSAK 201.69(d), not facts.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Treating a VAT refund claim by analogy with the 2019 agenda decision, which concerns a deposit on a disputed non-income tax, is a reading. An undisputed VAT overpayment is the same kind of statutory right, but the decision does not address it directly.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/1, PDF p. 11); Note 2a(ii) (Daftar 5/9, PDF p. 26); Note 3s (Daftar 5/68–5/70, PDF pp. 85–87); Note 43a–43d, 43f (Daftar 5/151–5/156, PDF pp. 168–173); Note 43g, PSC A (Natuna Sea) (Daftar 5/161–5/167, PDF pp. 178–184). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk; line figures checked against the PHE FS 2024 data extract).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting, IFRS Edition","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (Wiley), chapters on current liabilities and on accounting for income taxes, the textbook the lecture notes summarise; edition not checked. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes this series follows, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 19 – Accounting for Income Taxes\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): pretax financial income and taxable income, current tax and income tax payable, loss carryback with an income tax refund receivable (Garuda, Groh). NOTES. Carryback does not apply in Indonesia.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Lecture notes, \"Inter 2 – Chapter 13 – Current Liabilities\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan): income tax payable, \"kemungkinan restitusi pajak\" as a contingent asset. NOTES. Its Indonesian rates (25 and 12.5 percent) and its 1 April payment date were not used; its contingent-asset treatment of tax refunds is corrected in section 5. Neither note covers ISAK 123, Indonesian procedure, presentation or the industry cases; sections 4–8 follow the standards and laws named below.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pajak Penghasilan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 12), paras. 5, 12–14, 46, 71–73, 88. VERIFIED (web, AASB 112 text at standards.aasb.gov.au).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decisions \"Recognition of current income tax on uncertain tax position (IAS 12)\", July 2014 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org PDF); \"Interest and penalties related to income taxes\", 2017 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org project page); \"Deposits relating to taxes other than income tax (IAS 37)\", January 2019 (VERIFIED, ifrs.org agenda decision page).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ISAK 123 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Ketidakpastian dalam Perlakuan Pajak Penghasilan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRIC 23), paras. 10–11 and the full-knowledge assumption. VERIFIED (web, BDO and IFRS Foundation illustrative examples via search). PSAK 232 (IAS 32) AG12, statutory taxes not financial assets: VERIFIED (web, secondary: HM Treasury FRAB 128(03)). PSAK 237 (IAS 37) para. 5; PSAK 201 (IAS 1) paras. 56, 66, 69(d) (as amended for classification of liabilities, effective 2024), 122; PSAK 208 (IAS 8). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU KUP (UU 6/1983 as amended, latest by UU 6/2023 and UU 7/2021), art. 17B. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews; Ortax). Art. 25(8)–(9) and 27(5d), unpaid disputed tax and the 30/60 percent fines: VERIFIED (web, secondary: DDTCNews; pajak.com). UU 14/2002 on the Tax Court, art. 93(1), judicial review decided within 6 months: VERIFIED (web, pasal.id). Arts. 3, 17C–17D, 26–27, 27B: NOT VERIFIED (secondary sources via the 2026-10-09 recheck).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"UU PPh art. 6(2), five-year carryforward. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews). PMK 81/2024, taxes payable by upstream contractors. VERIFIED (web, DDTCNews summary) for the list of payment types only. PP 9/2022, 2.65 percent final tax on construction work by medium, large or specialist contractors: VERIFIED (web, pajak.go.id; hukumonline).","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of current tax assets from the year-end tax computation to the Tax Court, worked through PHE's tax note and set beside the industries with their own rules","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Current tax is the tax due on one year's taxable profit. When the tax already paid for a period exceeds the tax due, PSAK 212 recognises the excess as an asset, a current tax asset. In Indonesia it arises mainly from instalments and withholding, because the law allows no loss carryback.","The asset is measured at the amount expected to be recovered from the tax authority. Where the authority disputes the claim, ISAK 123 decides how much stays. The claim is kept in full if acceptance is probable and is otherwise measured at the most likely amount or the expected value. The contingent-asset rule of PSAK 237 does not apply to income tax.","The split between current and non-current follows the expected date of cash. A claim that goes through examination, objection and appeal can take years, so contested claims move into non-current assets."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = 'd7acd544-a882-41bc-b9de-68981b29ade8';
